<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.11.1: Changing the Graphing Window</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01442</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.11.1: Changing the Graphing Window</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cc3c4697-2bef-4e00-8166-dfe68c5d208f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para>Here is a graph of a function \(f(x)=400 \cdot (0.2)^x\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Graph of a function on grid, origin O. Horizontal axis, x, from 0 to 40 by 10's. Vertical axis, y, from 0 to 1,000, by 500's. Line crosses  y axis at 0 comma 400, quickly decreasing then running horizontal to x axis.">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_13_02_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Identify the approximate graphing window shown.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\(0&lt;x&lt;44\) and \(0&lt;y&lt;1,100\) or equivalent</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Suggest a new graphing window that would:
        <para>make the graph more informative or meaningful.</para>
       </para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\(
    0&lt;x&lt;10\) and \(0&lt;y&lt;450\). This window would include \(x\)-values where meaningful changes happen and exclude the parts where the changes are very small. The \(y\)-values are limited to values that matter, between 0 and 400, allowing us to see more clearly the changes in that range.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>make the graph less informative or meaningful.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>\(-10&lt;x&lt;80\) and \(10&lt;y&lt;100\). This window would show a lot of useless \(x\)-values and cut off the \(y\)-values at 100, which would make the behavior of the function when 𝑥 is between 0 and 1 very difficult to observe.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
